---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pageI18n } from '../i18n/ui';
import { personSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const { switchHref, alternates } = pageI18n(Astro.url.pathname);
const schema = [personSchema('en')];
---

<BaseLayout
  fullTitle="Igor Konovalov — Fullstack Developer in Belgrade, Serbia"
  description="About Igor Konovalov - fullstack developer in Belgrade building AI agents, Telegram bots, and creative-coding experiments."
  switchHref={switchHref}
  alternates={alternates}
  schema={schema}
>
  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-header">
      <h1 class="post-title">Igor Konovalov</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="post-content">
      <img
        src="/images/about/about_photo.jpg"
        alt="Igor Konovalov"
        class="about-photo"
      />

      <p>
        Hello! My name is Igor Konovalov — I'm a fullstack developer at EPAM,
        working mostly in TypeScript and Python, and I live in Belgrade, Serbia.
        Welcome to my personal site. I like building strange things, and lately
        the strangest and most interesting of them are AI agents.
      </p>

      <p>
        A lot of my recent work sits right at the boundary between solid
        engineering and letting an LLM agent actually drive. I built <a
          href="https://github.com/IgorKonovalov/market-analyzer"
          >market-analyzer</a
        >, a desktop trading-analysis app where <em
          >Claude Code is the control surface</em
        > — you talk to the agent, it calls tools on a local Python service, and a
        live chart reflects the results. Not a chat box bolted onto a GUI; the agent
        is the cockpit.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also build small, focused Telegram bots as real tools — a Serbian
        real-estate search bot that scrapes listing sites and tracks price
        changes, a channel-statistics bot with its own dashboard, a reference
        bot for traditional medicine. Each one does one thing and stays cheap
        and boring in production, which is my favourite kind of software.
      </p>

      <p>
        The one I'm most into right now is on the more playful side, and it's in
        Rust: <a href="https://github.com/IgorKonovalov/Ritmolux">Ritmolux</a>,
        a real-time music visualizer built around a shared Rust core (GPU
        rendering via <code>wgpu</code>) that feeds both a standalone app and a
        foobar2000 component. Both ship, the visuals are driven by editable text
        presets rather than code, and it has since picked up a second job:
        driving real stage lighting over Art-Net. There's a <a
          href="https://igorkonovalov.github.io/Ritmolux/">documentation site</a
        > with a gallery of every preset, and I wrote about <a
          href="/projects/2026/09/10/ritmolux_one_core_two_frontends/"
          >how it fits together</a
        >. It scratches the same itch as the generative-art demos on this site —
        mathematics and creative coding, and the geometry behind the pretty
        pictures.
      </p>

      <p>
        The newest one is <a href="https://github.com/IgorKonovalov/piano-tutor"
          >piano-tutor</a
        >, a desktop piano tutor that reads a MIDI keyboard, shows what you play
        as you play it, and gives per-bar feedback on a piece. It is early, and
        it exists mostly because I wanted to find out how quickly the working
        habits behind the other two would transfer to a new project.
      </p>

      <p>
        My hobby is photography. Follow me on <a
          href="https://www.instagram.com/some_strange/">Instagram</a
        >.
      </p>

      <p class="about-links">
        Find me on <a href="https://github.com/IgorKonovalov">GitHub</a> and <a
          href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/igor-konovalov/">LinkedIn</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2 class="virtual-tours-title">Virtual Tours</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/assets/FULL/BangTao_house/index.html">Bang Tao House</a>
        </li>
        <li><a href="/assets/FULL/vtour_A1/tour.html">Virtual Tour A1</a></li>
        <li>
          <a href="/assets/FULL/vtour_P1_fin/tour.html">Virtual Tour P1</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-photo {
    width: 33%;
    float: left;
    padding: 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-4) 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .virtual-tours-title {
    clear: both;
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 640px) {
    .about-photo {
      width: 100%;
      float: none;
      padding: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
    }
  }
</style>
